{
  "requirement": "The coverage workflow returns a failing exit status when test execution or\ncoverage collection fails and reports the failure.\nCollecting coverage does not turn a failing test run into a successful command.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.test-coverage.failures`. The requirement has two obligations: a failing exit status plus a report when execution or collection fails, and a failing test run must not become a successful command.\n\n**Test execution fails**\n- `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[real_parallel_processes_retries_child_profiles_and_filtered_reruns]` runs a real failing test (`retries` with `--retries 0`) through `habeas coverage`. Profiles are collected for that test. The test asserts `!failed.status.success()`, so collecting coverage does not turn the failing run into a success.\n- The same test checks that the saved run's outcome is `failed` and that the attempt outcome is `failed`. That is the report of the failure.\n- The same test also covers a compile error (`compile_error!`), which gives a failing status, and a build that fails before any test starts, which leaves the current set unchanged.\n- `a_process_that_cannot_start_is_failed_with_retained_attempt_metadata` asserts a failing status, a `failed` attempt outcome, and the diagnostic \"Cannot start test process\".\n- `execution_startup_failure_leaves_prior_selected_results_pending_without_measurements` asserts a failing status. It also asserts a `failed` run outcome and a \"No such file\" diagnostic.\n- `interrupting_the_parent_preserves_started_attempt_metadata` asserts exit code 130 and an `interrupted` outcome.\n\n**Coverage collection fails**\n- `passing_tests_with_missing_or_corrupt_profiles_fail_collection_and_retain_partial_reports` has all tests passing but a missing and a corrupt profile. It asserts a non-success status for both `coverage` and the full flow (`full_flow`). It asserts that stderr contains \"partial profiles and reports have been retained\". It asserts that the run outcome is `failed`. It also asserts that the per-attempt diagnostics record the failure. This is the key case where collection fails but the tests succeed.\n- `cleanup_failure_is_reported_and_next_collection_removes_orphans` asserts a failing status and a \"Permission denied\" message on stderr.\n- `unreadable_build_inputs_fail_preparation_before_any_tests_start` asserts a failing status and the message \"Coverage preparation failed\".\n- `a_build_that_cannot_start_preserves_the_current_set` and `a_failed_replacement_commit_reports_the_error_without_launching_execution` assert a failing status. The second also asserts that the synthetic commit error is reported on stderr.\n\nThe helpers `coverage`, `coverage_with_environment` and `full_flow` return the raw process `Output`. They do not mask the exit status.\n\n**Minor point**\nThe genuinely failing-test case asserts the report only through the saved `failed` outcome, not through stderr text. The saved failure record is a reasonable reading of \"reports the failure\", and the other failure classes assert stderr messages, so I did not treat this as a gap.",
  "references": "9bdf2ec8aecaed19a27baf6e8ceb29108016e4e095fa5dd9736b179bf57ea4f8",
